<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Bautista Topete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Bautista Topete was a Spanish admiral who raised the fleet at Cádiz against Isabella II in September 1868, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a>. He served as navy minister in the governments that followed. He was one of the main leaders of the revolution alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> and Francisco Serrano.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-topete.jpg" alt="Juan Bautista Topete" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Monleón · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JuanBatistaTopeteYCarballoPorRafaelMonle%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Bautista Topete y Carballo was born on 24 May 1821 in San Andrés Tuxtla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>; his father and grandfather had also been admirals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He entered the navy at the age of seventeen, cut out a Carlist vessel in 1839 and became a midshipman at twenty-two. He received the cross of naval merit for saving the life of a sailor in 1841 and became a lieutenant in 1845.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After three years on the West Indian station, where he was engaged in repressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, Topete was promoted frigate captain in 1857. He was chief of staff to the fleet in the Moroccan War of 1859 and afterwards received the cross of Saint Hermenegild among other honours. Appointed chief of the arsenal at Cádiz, he was elected a deputy and joined the Union Liberal of O'Donnell and Serrano.</p><p>Sent to the Pacific in command of the frigate Reina Blanca, he was present at the bombardments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, where he was badly wounded, and in other engagements of the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Made port captain at Cádiz on his return, he took the lead of the conspiracy in the fleet against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy. He sent the steamer Buenaventura to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> for Serrano and the other exiles, and when Prim and Sagasta arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> the stand of the fleet brought the people, garrison and authorities of Cádiz to its side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Topete held posts in the revolutionary government, among them that of minister of marine, was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> in 1869 and supported the claim of Antoine, Duke of Montpensier, to the throne. He at first opposed the election of Amadeus, but later sat in several cabinets of that king's reign. Prosecuted under the federal republic of 1873, he again took charge of the navy under Serrano in 1874.</p><p>After the Restoration he was kept aloof for many years, until in 1877 he accepted the presidency of a naval board. He later sat in the Senate as a life peer until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 29 October 1885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Bautista Topete” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1868 a military rising led by Generals Juan Prim and Francisco Serrano and Admiral <b>Juan Bautista Topete</b>, known as the Glorious Revolution, drove Isabella II into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Andrés Tuxtla, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.45,-95.21&amp;year=1862" aria-label="San Andrés Tuxtla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1970 1479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Bautista Topete"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2420 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2420 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.45,-95.21&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
